2        ANTHONY KABLE, MELISSA MILLS, AND DAVID J. WRIGHT

   A fundamental theorem about finite fields first asserted in [Gal30]
states that Gq = F×  q is a cyclic group of order q − 1. For a modern
treatment, see for example, [Gal20] ch. 21. For any divisor d | q − 1,
there is a unique subgroup Gq,d of Gq of order d consisting of all so-
lutions to xd = 1 in Fq . This subgroup may also be described as the
subgroup of q−1
              d
                 -th powers in Gq . Our main goal is the following the-
orem. Part (1) was proved by the second author in their 2006 masters
thesis ([Mil06]). To our knowledge, no other source describes caps in
AG(n, p) as subgroups of Fpn .
Theorem 1.        (1) For a, b, c ∈ G81,20 , a + b + c = 0 if and only if
      a = b = c.
  (2) For a, b, c ∈ G243,22 , a + b + c = 0 if and only if a = b = c.
  (3) For a, b, c ∈ G729,28 , a + b + c = 0 if and only if a = b = c.
  (4) For a, b, c, d ∈ G22n ,2n +1 , a + b + c + d = 0 if and only if, after
      renaming if necessary, a = b and c = d.
  (5) For even n, G22n ,2n +1 ∪ {0}is a cap set of size 2n + 2 in F22n .
   Thus, Theorem 1 part (1) implies the subgroup G81,20 is a cap set of
size 20 in F81 , which is shown to be the maximal cap size in AG(4, 3) in
[Pel70] as well as in [DM03]. Similarly, Theorem 1 part (4) implies the
subgroup G64,9 is a cap set of size 9 in F64 . The paper [CFG+ 23] proves
this is the maximum size of a cap in AG(6, 2). In both F81 and F64 ,
it has been proved there is only one maximal cap up to affine linear
isomorphism.
   Part (2) of Theorem 1 asserts that G243,22 is a cap in F243 . It is proved
in [EFLS02] that a maximal cap in AG(5, 3) has 45 elements. We shall
also prove that G243,22 is a complete cap, meaning it is not contained
in any larger cap set, and that G243,22 is the smallest complete cap.
   Part (3) implies G729,28 is a cap of size 28 in F729 . Potechin proved a
maximal cap in AG(6, 3) has 112 = 4 · 28 elements (see [Pot08]). With
the aid of computer algebra, we can show the union of certain pairs
of cosets, for instance, G729,28 ∪ x G729,28 , is a cap set of size 56. The
Potechin cap is not a union of 4 cosets.
   A Sidon set S in an abelian group is defined by the property that, if
a + b = c + d for a, b, c, d ∈ S with a ̸= b and c ̸= d, then {a, b} = {c, d}.
For the group Zn2 ≈ F2n , Sidon sets are the same as cap sets. Theorem
1 part (4) implies G22n ,2n +1 is a Sidon set in F22n , and for even n part
(5) implies G22n ,2n +1 ∪ {0} is also a Sidon set in F22n of size 2n + 2.
Sidon sets of these sizes in AG(2n, 2) are constructed in [Nag25] by
means of conics in AG(2, 2n ). It is proved in [CGR25] that a maximal
cap in AG(7, 2) has size 12, and computer calculations in [CP24] show
that a maximal cap in AG(8, 2) has size 18 = 24 + 2.
              SUBGROUPS OF FINITE FIELDS AS CAP SETS                     3

   The game SET is a popular pattern-matching card game invented
by geneticist Marsha Jean Falco in 1974 and first marketed in 1990.
The connection between this game and the affine geometry AG(4, 3)
has been discussed in quite a few sources; see [MGGG17] for example.
The game EvenQuads was developed at Bard College out of the 2013
undergraduate senior project of Jeffrey Pereira ([Per13]) supervised by
Lauren Rose. See [Ros23] for more information about EvenQuads and
its connection to AG(6, 2).
   While discussing the work [DM03] on the mathematics of the game
of SET in 2005, the first author suggested that the subgroup of order
20 in F× 81 might be a cap set, and this idea was proved by the second
author in their masters thesis [Mil06]. When we learned about the
new game of EvenQuads, we discovered the same phenomenon held for
F64 . In the meantime, several papers [FKM+ 14] and [AFLM22] have
appeared discussing the partition of AG(4, 3) as the union of 4 disjoint
maximal cap sets with one point left over. The earlier paper [Tuc07]
gave a partition of AG(4, 3) as a disjoint union of 9 cap sets of size
9; this paper modeled AG(4, 3) as the two-dimensional affine geometry
AG(2, 9) over F9 and identified the 9 point caps as conics. We should
like to point out that, if a subgroup G ⊆ F×
                                           q is a cap set, then each coset
xG is clearly also a cap set by the linearity of the defining equation.
This establishes the following:

Corollary 2.      (1) The cosets of G81,20 form a partition of F× 81 into
      4 maximal cap sets.
  (2) The cosets of G243,22 form a partition of F×  243 into 11 complete
      cap sets of size 22.
  (3) The cosets of G729,28 form a partition of F×729 into 26 cap sets of
      size 28. These cosets may be paired to obtain a partition into
      13 cap sets of size 56.
  (4) The cosets of G22n ,2n +1 form a partition of F×          n
                                                      22n into 2 − 1 cap
      sets of size 2n + 1.

   The fact that subgroups of F×    q are related to cap sets is perhaps
another example of the theme expressed in [Kal25] that “multiplica-
tively structured sets cannot exhibit significant additive structure.”
Hill also observes in [Hil76] that known maximal caps have automor-
phism groups that act transitively on the cap. The results in this paper
are a beginning exploration of the questions of which subgroups of F×   q
are cap sets, and whether or not the equation xd1 + · · · + xdk = 0 over
Fq , for d | q − 1, implies that two powers xdj must be equal. In general,
though, the number of affine k-flats in a proper subgroup of F×   q seems
4       ANTHONY KABLE, MELISSA MILLS, AND DAVID J. WRIGHT

to be quite small relative to the number of affine k-flats in Fq . This
may be a good topic for further investigation.
  There have been quite a few contributions to the question of maxi-
mum sizes of cap sets in AG(n, q). For more information on the known
values and on bounds of various kinds, see, for example, [Ben19, Ede04,
EFLS02, EG17, Gro19, Hil73, Hil76, Hil83, TW21, Tha21, Tha20], and
the many references within these articles.

                 2. Proof of Theorem 1, Part 1
Proof. Assume there are distinct x, y, z ∈ F× 81 satisfying x + y + z = 0
and x20 = y 20 = z 20 = 1. Sincez is nonzero,   we may divide by z to
         x      y                x 20    y 20
                                          
obtain z + z + 1 = 0 and z            = z      = 1. Then we may as well
assume z = 1 in our proof and suppose x + y + 1 = 0 and x20 = y 20 = 1.
  In characteristic 3, we have (x + y)3 = x3 + y 3 , and by repetition that
implies (x + y)27 = x27 + y 27 . Applying this identity to the equation
x + 1 = −y with x20 = y 20 = 1, we obtain
      (x + 1)27 = x27 + 1 = x7 + 1 = (−y)27 = (−y)7 = (x + 1)7 .
The binomial expansion of (x + 1)7 − x7 − 1 = 0 modulo 3 reduces
to x6 − x4 − x3 + x = 0. This factors as 0 = x(x2 − 1)(x3 − 1) =
x(x + 1)(x − 1)4 . Since x ̸= 0, this implies x = ±1. If x = −1,
x + y + 1 = 0 implies y = 0 contradicting y 20 = 1. If x = 1, then
x + y + 1 = 0 implies y = 1 = x. This completes the proof that G81,20
is a cap set, as well as the stronger statement that, if x + y + z = 0 for
x, y, z ∈ G81,20 , then x = y = z.                                      □

                 3. Proof of Theorem 1, Part 2
Proof. As in the previous proof, we may assume there are x, y ∈ F× 243
such that if x + y + 1 = 0 and x22 = y 22 = 1. Taking the 27-th power,
we have
      (x + 1)27 = x27 + 1 = x5 + 1 = (−y)27 = (−y)5 = (x + 1)5 .
The binomial expansion mod 3 of (x + 1)5 − x5 − 1 = 0 then yields
−x4 + x3 + x2 − x = 0, which factors as −x(x + 1)(x − 1)2 = 0. Since
x ̸= 0 is assumed, and since x = −1 and x+y +1 = 0 implies y = 0, the
only valid possibility is x = 1. Then x+y+1 = 0 implies y = 1 = x. □
  This produces a cap set G22 = G243,22 of 22 elements of F×      243 . We
show next this is a complete cap. To prove a cap set C is complete, we
must show that for any x ∈   / C, there exist distinct y, z ∈ C such that
x+y +z = 0. In that case, we say x is represented by C. The analogous
idea is valid in characteristic 2 as well if we define w to be represented
                SUBGROUPS OF FINITE FIELDS AS CAP SETS                         5

by G if there are distinct x, y, z ∈ G such that w + x + y + z = 0. The
next theorem reduces the calculations necessary to prove a subgroup is
a complete cap.
Theorem 3. For a prime power q and divisor d | q − 1, suppose the
subgroup G of d-th powers in F×   q is a cap in the sense that there are no
solutions to x1 + · · · + xm = 0 with distinct x1 , . . . , xm ∈ G. An element
y∈ / G is represented by G if there are distinct x1 , . . . , xm−1 ∈ G with
y + x1 + · · · + xm−1 = 0. We say G is complete if every y ∈            / G is
represented by G. Let g be a primitive root of Fq . Then G is complete
if 0 and all powers g k for 1 ≤ k ≤ d − 1 are represented by G.
Proof. We must prove every x ∈  / G is represented by G. By hypothesis,
we may assume x = g k for some integer d ≤ k ≤ q − 2 and d ∤ k. By
division, k = ad + r for integers a and r with 1 ≤ r ≤ d − 1. Since we
assumed g r is represented by G, we have:
                        g r + g b1 d + · · · + g bm−1 d = 0,
for integers b1 , . . . , bm−1 . Multiplying by g ad shows g k = g ad+r is repre-
sented by G:
                  g ad+r + g (b1 +a)d + · · · + g (bm−1 +a)d = 0.
Thus, G is complete.                                                          □
Theorem 4. G243,22 is a complete cap in F243 of the smallest possible
size.
Proof. Since G = G243,22 is the subgroup of 11-th powers, by our previ-
ous theorem we must show that 0 and g k , 1 ≤ k ≤ 10, are represented
by G. A primitive root g of F×  243 has order 242 = 2 · 121, and so g
                                                                        121

has order 2 and is then equal to −1. Then 0 + 1 + g 121 = 0, which
proves 0 is represented by G.
   To represent g k for 1 ≤ k ≤ 10, we note that g 5 − g + 1 is irreducible
mod 3. By computing the remainders of g k (mod g 5 −g +1) mod 3 (for
this we used the computer algebra system Maple ([Map25]), although
it is not hard to do by hand), we can confirm that g has order 242.
Working modulo g 5 − g + 1, we may find the following equations:
            g + g 55 + g 154 = 0,               g 2 + g 154 + g 220 = 0,
           g 3 + g 165 + g 220 = 0,             g 4 + g 22 + g 55 = 0,
           g 5 + g 11 + g 22 = 0,               g 6 + g 176 + g 220 = 0,
           g 7 + g 33 + g 165 = 0,              g 8 + 1 + g 220 = 0,
           g 9 + g 11 + g 176 = 0,             g 10 + 1 + g 88 = 0.
6       ANTHONY KABLE, MELISSA MILLS, AND DAVID J. WRIGHT

These prove each g k is represented by G for 1 ≤ k ≤ 10, and completes
the proof that G is complete. Since a cap of size 21 or smaller can
                                 21
represent by addition at most 2 + 21 = 231 < 243 elements, there
are no smaller complete caps.                                       □

