  All the theorems in this paper may be verified by relatively simple
and fast computer algebra calculations. If g is a primitive root of F×
                                                                     q
and if its minimal polynomial is poly = p(x) = 0, the computer math-
ematics system Maple ([Map25]), for instance, has a fast polynomial
remainder procedure rem which allows us to test
         rem(x^(i) + x^(j) + x^(k), poly,x) mod p = 0
and thus determine if {g i , g j , g k } is an affine line. The authors are
happy to share a basic Maple worksheet that confirms our theorems.
We have also confirmed these theorems with the finite fields package in
SAGE (see [The26]). In addition, we used computer algebra to verify
that the disjoint union of cosets of G26 ∪ x G26 is a cap set of size 56.
However, the 112-cap of Potechin is not a union of 4 cosets. The pairs
of cosets Pk = xk G26 ∪ xk+1 G26 for 0 ≤ k ≤ 12 form a partition of F×  729
by 13 cap sets of size 56.

